# Time trade-off and standard gamble: whose values, and what lies below zero?
next: {"title":"Severity: absolute shortfall, proportional shortfall, or something else?","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/health-technology-assessment-deciding/severity-absolute-shortfall-proportional.cast"}

## Time Trade-Off, Step by Step
voice: female
level: basic
use: number_line
with:
    min: 0
    max: 10
    step: 1
    points:
      - {at: 10, label: 'Life A: 10 years'}
      - {at: 6, label: 'Life B: 6 years'}
    intervals:
      - {from: 0, to: 6, label: 6 years in full health}
      - {from: 6, to: 10, label: 4 years given up}
    arrow_ops:
      - {from: 10, to: 5, label: 5?}
      - {from: 5, to: 7, label: 7?}
      - {from: 7, to: 6, label: '6'}

Now that we have seen the two instruments side by side, let's run one of them properly — because the single number it produces will multiply every year of life in the nusinersen model.
    draw line tick_0 tick_1 tick_2 tick_3 tick_4 tick_5 tick_6 tick_7 tick_8 tick_9 tick_10 parallel true

Life A is fixed: ten years in the SMA health state we described, and then death.
    draw pt_0 pt_0_label

Against it stands Life B — full health, but shorter — and the interviewer hunts for the length at which you stop caring which one you get.
    point at.ref pt_0 gesture tap

First offer: five years in full health instead of ten in the state. Most respondents refuse — five is too much life to hand over.
    draw hop_0 hop_0_label

So the interviewer swings back up: seven years of full health, and now Life B is clearly the better deal.
    draw hop_1 hop_1_label

Down, up, down — the offers close in from both sides until the choice becomes genuinely hard.
    draw hop_2 hop_2_label

At six years the respondent cannot choose. That indifference is the measurement; everything before it was only the search.
    draw pt_1 pt_1_label

    pause 0.5

Six years in full health, weighed as exactly equal to ten years in the SMA state.
    draw interval_0 interval_0_label

Divide the shorter healthy life by the longer sick one: u equals six over ten, zero point six.
    text equation "u = x / t = 6 / 10 = 0.6" x 500 y 620 font_size 36

A year in this state counts as sixty percent of a healthy year.
    mark eq_box target equation kind box
    delivery grave

    quiz "The next respondent is indifferent at 8 years instead of 6. What is u?"
        intro: A quick check before we go on.
        right: u is 8 over 10, or 0.8 — giving up fewer years means the state is judged closer to full health.
        wrong: Read it as the ratio of the two lifetimes: eight divided by ten.
        + 0.8
        * 0.2
        * 1.25

    clear keep ["line","tick_0","tick_1","tick_2","tick_3","tick_4","tick_5","tick_6","tick_7","tick_8","tick_9","tick_10","pt_0","pt_1","interval_0","equation","eq_box","pt_0_label","pt_1_label","interval_0_label"]

But look at which years were sacrificed: never the next four — always the last four, the ones furthest away.
    draw interval_1 interval_1_label

Distant years feel cheap, because we are impatient — so an impatient respondent hands over more of them, and the ratio lands lower than the state really deserves.
    focus interval_1

One camp draws the obvious conclusion: an uncorrected time trade-off measures impatience as much as health, so the utilities should be adjusted upward.
    highlight equation effect glow

The other answers that the QALY calculation already discounts future years at four percent, so correcting inside the utility counts the same impatience twice — and no one agrees on the rate to correct with.
    point at.ref equation gesture underline

    quiz "If respondents discount distant years, what does the raw ratio x over t do to the utility?"
        right: It understates it — cheap distant years get traded away freely, so x comes out too low.
        wrong: Think about which years get sacrificed: the far-off ones, which already count for less.
        + Understates it
        * Overstates it
        * Leaves it unchanged

And this haggling is not a classroom exercise: the EQ-5D value sets that price health states in Norway and across Europe are built from thousands of interviews shaped exactly like this one.
    highlight pt_1 effect glow

So the time trade-off answers a health state with a length of life: zero point six is not a feeling, it is a ratio of two lifetimes.

## Whose Values Count?
voice: female
level: basic
use: two_by_two_table
with:
    title: Valuing SMA type 2 — illustrative numbers
    row_label: Who was asked
    col_label: Same state, same instrument
    row_values:
      - General public
      - Patients & families
    col_values:
      - Value of the state
      - Five extra years
    cells:
      - [u = 0.35, 1.75 QALYs]
      - [u = 0.60, 3.00 QALYs]
    cell_notes:
      - [imagining the state, 5 × 0.35]
      - [living in the state, 5 × 0.60]
    highlight:
      - [0, 0]
      - [0, 1]
    box:
      x: 60
      'y': 150
      w: 880
      h: 585
sources:
    - id: harris1987
      title: QALYfying the value of life
      authors: Harris
      year: 1987
      finding: The 'double jeopardy' objection to QALYs

    mark pick target row_header_0 kind box hidden true

Now that we have seen why the standard gamble reads higher than the time trade-off, a harder question: the instrument is settled, but whose answers get written into the tariff — and that choice can halve a drug's measured benefit.
    draw title grid

    draw row_title col_title row_header_0 row_header_1 col_header_0

Ask a representative sample of Norwegian citizens to imagine life with SMA type two — powered wheelchair, help with dressing, ventilation at night — and their time trade-off answers land, in round illustrative numbers, near zero point three five.
    draw cell_0_0

    pause 0.3

Ask the people and families who actually live in that state, and the same instrument, on the same description, gives something closer to zero point six.
    draw cell_1_0

That gap of twenty-five utility points is wider than the gain most treatments ever deliver.
    point at.ref cell_1_0 gesture circle

Patients adapt — they rebuild a life around the state and quietly recalibrate the scale they are answering on; the literature calls it response shift.
    focus cell_1_0 row_header_1

The public are imagining, and imagination fixes on what is lost — the wheelchair fills the frame while the friendships, the schooling, the ordinary Tuesday drop out of it. That is the focusing illusion.
    focus cell_0_0 row_header_0

    pause 0.4

Now watch what that difference does to an actual QALY calculation.
    draw col_header_1

Say nusinersen buys a child five extra years in that state: valued at zero point three five, those years are worth one point seven five QALYs.
    draw cell_0_1

Valued at zero point six, the very same five years are worth three QALYs — nearly double the benefit, for exactly the same drug at exactly the same price.
    draw cell_1_1

    quiz "Using public values rather than patient values, a treatment that only adds years in that state looks…" intro "Quick check."
        right: Less — each added year is multiplied by a smaller utility, so the same five years buy fewer QALYs.
        wrong: The other way round: a lower utility scales every added year down, so the QALY gain shrinks.
        * More cost-effective
        + Less cost-effective
        * Unchanged

And yet Norwegian guidance, like NICE's, buys the top row: health states described by patients, but valued by a sample of the general public.
    draw hl_0_0 hl_0_1 pick

Not because citizens judge the state better — the people living it plainly know it best — but because they fund the budget, bear the care displaced elsewhere, and could wake up in that state tomorrow.
    highlight row_header_0 effect glow

    quiz "So why do the guidelines use general-population values?"
        right: The public pay for the budget and could end up in any state themselves — it is a claim about standing, not about accuracy.
        wrong: Not accuracy — patients know the state far better; it is that the public fund the budget and bear the displaced care.
        * The public judge health states more accurately
        + The public fund the system and bear the risk
        * Patients refuse to answer TTO

The objection is old and unresolved: the philosopher John Harris called it double jeopardy — you are unlucky enough to live in the state, and then your extra years are counted at a discount the healthy majority set for you.
    highlight cell_1_1 effect pulse

Defenders answer that adapted values would punish exactly the states people cope with best — a system paying least to fix what patients have learned to live with.
    highlight cell_0_1 effect pulse

So a utility is never a property of the illness alone; it is the illness plus the person we chose to ask — and that choice was made long before any clinical evidence arrived.
    delivery grave

